# Git's text-vs-binary heuristic misdetects PDFs: it samples the first 8k
# for NUL bytes, and a PDF whose leading objects are ASCII looks like text.
# On a Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true) every LF then becomes CRLF,
# which shifts every byte offset in the file. The xref table and the
# startxref pointer are byte offsets, so the file silently decodes as
# "incorrect startxref pointer" and only survives because readers fall
# back to rebuilding the xref. Committing such a working copy back
# normalizes CRLF to LF and stores a genuinely corrupt PDF.
#
# sample/evidence-packet.pdf is the artifact an auditor extracts
# packet.json from, so it must round-trip byte-exactly on every platform.
*.pdf binary

# Evidence packets are checked out on Windows and hashed on Linux CI.
# Canonicalization parses the JSON, so line endings do not change the
# manifest hash, but pinning LF keeps the working copy identical to the
# blob and stops packet.json showing as perpetually modified.
*.json text eol=lf

# test.sh runs on ubuntu-latest; CRLF would break the shebang.
*.sh text eol=lf
